{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lotion Play is a subset of the better known Wet-And-Messy fetish (WAM), which typically involves participants using food (such as pudding or whipped cream), mud, or paint as a lubricant to facilitate sexual activity.","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Lotion Play isolates lotion specifically as a lubricating medium - setting it apart in the Wet-and-Messy genre, as other common WAM mediums do not have such specific popularity as Lotion Play.","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Lotion Play (ローションプレイ , rōshon purei ) , also known as gookkake, gluekkake, is a popular fetish, form of Japanese erotica and prostitution request involving the use of copious amounts of lubricant, which in the Japanese language is referred to by the Old-French word "lotion" (ローション in Japanese).","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":"Typically lotion play involves a participant rubbing lotion on another using their body, sexual intercourse in a pool or bath filled with lotion, or lotion being poured over the participants during sex.","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In Japan (and other parts of the world), lotion is available in concentrated form (e.g.: liter/gallon) which can be added to hot water to produce the desired amount of lotion.","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" A 1-gallon concentrate will typically yield 6-10 gallons of lotion (J-Lube Lotion Concentrate).","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The main component in most lotion is polyacrylate.","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" A similar effect can be achieved by dissolving powdered methyl cellulose in water.","title":"Lotion play"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Yameen is a hiphop producer from Philadelphia, PA.","title":"Yameen"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His most recent work, "Come On & Go Off" was released on September 2nd, 2014 on Rumble Pack Records.","title":"Yameen"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His music can be heard weekly on the Activision videogames podcast, One of Swords where he is also occasionally a guest commentator.","title":"Yameen"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The sural nerve is a sensory nerve in the calf region (sura) of the leg.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is made up of collateral branches of the tibial nerve and common fibular nerve.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Two cutaneous branches, the medial and lateral, form the sural nerve.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The medial cutaneous branch arises from the tibial nerve, and the lateral cutaneous branch arises from the common fibular nerve.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The tibial nerve and the common fibular nerve arise as the sciatic nerve divides into two branches in the popliteal fossa.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" As the tibial nerve travels down the popliteal fossa, and before it goes beneath the gastrocnemius, it gives off a cutaneous branch which is the medial sural cutaneous nerve.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" This nerve courses laterally over the lateral head of the gastrocnemius.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The common fibular nerve also gives off a small cutaneous branch which is the lateral sural cutaneous nerve.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" When the common fibular nerve is divided from the sciatic nerve, it travels parallel to the distal portion of the biceps femoris muscle and towards the fibular head.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" The small cutaneous branch arises as the common fibular nerve travels towards the fibular head.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" The nerve then continues down the leg on the posterior-lateral side, then posterior to the lateral malleolus where it runs deep to the fibularis tendon sheath and reaches the lateral tuberosity of the fifth toe, where it ramifies.","title":"Sural nerve"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Guitar Hero 5 (initially referred to as Guitar Hero V) is a music rhythm game and the fifth main entry in the "Guitar Hero" series.","title":"Guitar Hero 5"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The game was developed by Neversoft and published by Activision, and released internationally in September 2009 for the Xbox 360, PlayStation 2, 3 and Wii consoles.","title":"Guitar Hero 5"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Similar to the preceding title, "Guitar Hero World Tour", "Guitar Hero 5" is geared towards playing in a four-person band experience, including lead and bass guitar, drums, and vocals.","title":"Guitar Hero 5"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The game is available as a standalone title, allowing players to use existing compatible instrument controllers, and as a bundle that provides these controllers.","title":"Guitar Hero 5"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" "Guitar Hero 5" adds several new features, such as drop-in/drop-out play, bands composed of any combination of available instruments, a Rockfest competitive mode consisting of several various scoring mechanisms, and both song-specific and general Challenges to unlock new avatars, clothing, and other extras in the game.","title":"Guitar Hero 5"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Many of these changes were added to make the game a more social experience, allowing players across a range of skill levels to be able to play cooperatively and competitively against each other both locally and online.","title":"Guitar Hero 5"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Guitar Hero series (sometimes referred to as the Hero series) is a series of music rhythm games first published in 2005 by RedOctane and Harmonix, and distributed by Activision, in which players use a guitar-shaped game controller to simulate playing lead, bass guitar, and rhythm guitar across numerous rock music songs.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Players match notes that scroll on-screen to colored fret buttons on the controller, strumming the controller in time to the music in order to score points, and keep the virtual audience excited.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The games attempt to mimic many features of playing a real guitar, including the use of fast-fingering hammer-ons and pull-offs and the use of the whammy bar to alter the pitch of notes.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Most games support single player modes, typically a Career mode to play through all the songs in the game, and both competitive and cooperative multiplayer modes.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" With the introduction of "Guitar Hero World Tour" in 2008, the game includes support for a four-player band including vocals and drums.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The series initially used mostly cover versions of songs created by WaveGroup Sound, but most recent titles feature soundtracks that are fully master recordings, and in some cases, special re-recordings, of the songs.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Later titles in the series feature support for downloadable content in the form of new songs.","title":"Guitar Hero"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Right Back at It Again" is the second track and the first single from A Day to Remember's fifth album, "Common Courtesy" (2013).","title":"Right Back at It Again"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In October 20, 2015, the song was featured in Activision rhythm-music game, "".","title":"Right Back at It Again"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: music
